# Agent Contract For {{PROJECT_NAME}}

This repository uses ToVA Project Workflow generation 2 as its documentation, planning, verification, and continuity layer. The workflow supports the product; it does not redefine the product.

Start with `{{OWNER_bootProtocol}}`. `{{OWNER_executionState}}` owns durable machine-readable execution/writer state. `{{OWNER_currentTask}}` is the sole live agent work board for approved bounded work, paused work needing an owner decision, and the next ready Slice. It is human-readable so the human owner can understand, steer, and authorize agent work; it is not a task list for the human to perform. `{{OWNER_currentState}}` provides compact orientation and pointers, never a second task board; `{{OWNER_docsMap}}` maps durable document ownership. `{{OWNER_repositoryIndexGuide}}` and `{{OWNER_repositoryIndex}}` provide navigation-only routes to exact owners/source/tests/proof without duplicating their truth. `{{OWNER_workModel}}` owns Campaign/Slice definitions, `{{OWNER_collaborationProtocol}}` owns online/local/multi-node coordination, and `{{OWNER_projectHealth}}` owns proportional verification. `{{OWNER_handoffs}}` owns transfer conventions and `{{OWNER_outputs}}` owns retained non-handoff work-product conventions. `{{OWNER_architecture}}` owns current construction, `{{OWNER_designLanguage}}` owns visual/UI reuse plus user-facing language and copy, Capability owners describe end-to-end outcomes, and Feature owners inventory concrete mechanisms. `{{OWNER_tovaHelp}}` explains that routing. During deployment/adoption/upgrade, `{{OWNER_tovaSetup}}` owns setup-to-acceptance and `{{OWNER_tovaMigration}}` owns reconciliation semantics; while project definition remains open, `{{OWNER_projectDiscovery}}` owns the short evidence-first interview.

At each user-facing startup, inspect only the `User Introduction` section in `{{OWNER_currentState}}`. Follow the agent introduction protocol in `{{OWNER_tovaHelp}}`: give the short beginner walkthrough once for `not_shown` or a missing marker, persist `shown` only after actual delivery, and record `skipped` only when the user explicitly declines. Preserve the marker across sessions and upgrades; never automatically repeat for `shown` or `skipped`. Always answer requested help. This presentation preference is not acceptance or an extra work gate.

Before editing:

1. Read Execution State and Current Task; check the User Introduction marker below and load the rest of Current State only when broader context is needed.
2. Confirm the requested Slice and likely source owner.
3. Use the smallest matching repository route when the Slice or source layout is unfamiliar; fall back to direct-source inspection if the route is stale or contradictory.
4. Preserve existing project content, casing, architecture, and commands.
5. Read `{{OWNER_designLanguage}}` before changing user-visible design, reusable UI surfaces, interaction text, or product copy.
6. Edit durable source rather than generated output.
7. Run the gate named by Project Health.

Verification economy is a hard execution rule: for an ordinary Slice, use the smallest focused proof that can detect a regression introduced by that Slice. Do not automatically run broad regression, full UI/device, migration, performance, accessibility, or release suites at every Slice boundary. Those cumulative checks belong at Campaign or release acceptance unless a later Slice depends on them, the actual blast radius justifies them, a concrete failure gives reason to broaden, or the owner explicitly asks. More testing is permitted when technically justified; more testing is not automatically more diligent.

Keep active or paused work in one clearly named section of `{{OWNER_currentTask}}`. Preserve an unfinished paused checklist there; when it completes, remove or collapse it and place detailed history in Git, `{{OWNER_docChangeLog}}`, archives, or the relevant truth owner. Do not update broad status, feature/capability, architecture, handoff, or change-log documents for every small step.

Before creating or redirecting a build root, classify its exact path under the Work Model. Prefer shared/reused roots. Use a uniquely named Slice-ephemeral root only when isolation is required, record its cleanup action before creation, and remove only that exact Slice-owned path after final evidence. Gitignored never means safe to delete; intentionally retained history, dependencies, shared caches, unknown paths, and another active owner's output remain under their normal owners.

On the first agent run after installation, inspect the repository and current owners before asking the discovery questions. If the user is unsure, offer two or three evidence-based options plus one conservative default, explain the tradeoffs briefly, and label suggestions as assumptions until the user confirms them.

## Migration Mode

During initial adoption or a version upgrade, the current supported Starter Kit owns ToVA workflow semantics while the project continues to own project facts, commands, compatible stronger rules, history, and evidence. Follow `{{OWNER_tovaMigration}}`, review the migration ledger, and do not retire an old owner until every unique current fact has an accepted destination. Keep workflow migration separate from new product behavior unless a bounded repair is required to preserve an existing check or behavior.

Keep completed history in Git and milestone logs. Do not turn current state, Execution State, Notes, or Exchanges into completed-work archives. Keep volatile Action progress in a PR checkpoint or ignored `.tova-runtime/` journal. Stop before destructive operations, dependency expansion, baseline promotion, publication, or work outside the authorized Slice unless the owner has approved it.

Use `{{OWNER_handoffs}}` only for explicit AI-to-AI, AI-to-Codex, cross-session, cross-machine, or human transfers. Handoffs package and point to current truth; they do not become a second task board, status owner, history system, or acceptance record. Exclude secrets and local-only artifacts, and verify archives before transfer.

When the owner says work will be handed to ChatGPT, Claude, a GitHub agent, a local model, another Codex session, or any AI without reliable prior context—or asks for more context for assigned Slices—treat it as an explicit cold-agent handoff request. Follow `{{OWNER_handoffs}}`. Create or reuse a revision-backed Campaign Context Capsule from `{{OWNER_campaignContextTemplate}}` and prepare one bounded Slice Execution Packet per assigned Slice from `{{OWNER_sliceExecutionPacketTemplate}}`. Use `compact`, `expanded`, or `auto` as requested; if unspecified, prefer `expanded` for a cold agent.

Use `{{OWNER_agentNotes}}` only for sparse non-authoritative observations. Serial work keeps one active branch and moves one writer lease; parallel source writers use distinct branches and isolated checkouts, while several nodes may verify one exact revision read-only.

Use `{{OWNER_outputs}}` for useful reports, analyses, exports, or other intentionally retained work products that are not transfer packages and do not belong in a stronger owner. Outputs point to current truth rather than replacing it, and generated/build artifacts do not become durable merely by being copied there.
